WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:07.940
MARTIN NALLY: Welcome
to Google Next,

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:13.860
and welcome to this talk
on designing quality APIs.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.610
I'm glad to see a
reasonably good turnout.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.900
I did once give a presentation
here in San Francisco,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.150
and I was scheduled in a
back room at the same time

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.170
as the keynote presentation with
some reasonably famous speaker.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.030
I can't remember who it was.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.500
And when it came time
to start the session,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.450
there was exactly one
person in the audience

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.334
sitting in the front row.

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:38.750
And I was just
sort of considering

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.260
what to do at this point
when some fellow wandered in

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.880
through the back
door, and it was

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.740
fairly obvious from
the way he was behaving

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.010
that he was completely lost.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.500
He didn't know where he was.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.480
So I give him an
extremely warm welcome

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.202
and thanked him for
showing up to my session.

00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:56.410
So of course at this point,
this poor fellow's stuck.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.279
I've sort of cut off
his line of retreat.

00:00:59.279 --> 00:01:01.820
So anyway, the three of us sat
in the front row and just sort

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.620
of chit-chatted for an hour.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.170
So I'm very happy
that so many of you

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.476
have turned up for this talk.

00:01:11.754 --> 00:01:14.170
So I assume you're here because
you're interested in APIs.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.545
And if you're
interested in APIs,

00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:18.460
you'll know that there's lots
of controversy around APIs,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.330
and there's lots of conflicting
advice on what to do.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.266
My experience is
when that happens,

00:01:24.266 --> 00:01:26.140
it's often the case that
people aren't really

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.370
disagreeing about technology.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:31.450
The reason that they disagree
is that they come to the problem

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.899
with different ideas
and different values.

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:36.190
They have different objectives.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.650
They're trying to solve
different problems.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.450
And so since they're
actually trying

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.825
to solve different
problems, they

00:01:41.825 --> 00:01:45.460
tend to disagree
about the solutions.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.010
So I thought I'd take a
moment to talk about some

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.010
of the different things
that people are actually

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.160
trying to achieve
when they approach

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.890
the problem of
developing an API.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.560
This is a picture
of Richard Hamming.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.268
You've probably heard
of Richard Hamming.

00:02:01.268 --> 00:02:03.860
He's a famous person
in computer circles.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.460
He was a sort of
mathematician and scientist

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.375
of the 20th century.

00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:09.580
He is famous for
Hamming codes, which

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.030
are error correction and
detection codes which

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.280
are actually still used today.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.905
They're used in RAM
memory, and they're

00:02:15.905 --> 00:02:18.884
used in wireless protocols.

00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:20.800
One of the things that
Hamming was famous for,

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.200
in addition to Hamming
windows, Hamming distance,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.229
Hamming codes, he was famous
for a series of lectures

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:26.770
that he gave.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.936
And you can find those
lectures on the internet now.

00:02:28.936 --> 00:02:32.170
There's versions on YouTube
as well as written versions.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:35.710
And one of the most famous is
called "You and Your Research,"

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.410
which is his advice
to researchers

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.010
on how to maximize their impact
and manage their careers.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.640
And Hamming himself says that
although his experience is

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.540
from research, his advice
is useful in different kinds

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.830
of domains.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.030
So Hamming makes some important
points, some useful points.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.699
If you really want
to have an impact,

00:02:56.699 --> 00:02:58.240
then you should make
sure that you're

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.800
working on problems
that are really

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.300
important problems
in your field.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.300
If you're not working on
the important problems,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.660
it's unlikely that you
will do important work.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.164
Another point that he makes--
in fact, this is a great talk.

00:03:10.164 --> 00:03:12.080
If you're not familiar
with Hamming's lecture,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.840
then you should go and spend
some time looking at it.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.316
In fact, the truth is if you
only had one hour to spend,

00:03:17.316 --> 00:03:19.690
you'd probably do better to
spend it listening to Hamming

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:20.564
than listening to me.

00:03:20.564 --> 00:03:23.020
But I'm going to assume that
you have two hours to spend.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.061
So you're going to spend
one hour listening to me

00:03:25.061 --> 00:03:27.924
and then one hour
listening to Hamming.

00:03:27.924 --> 00:03:29.590
The other thing that
Hamming talks about

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.120
is this idea of having
an attack on a problem.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:37.090
And for him, an attack is kind
of a plausible approach that

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.090
might lead to a solution to
the problem or, at least,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.340
make a dent in the problem.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.150
And in fact, he says that
the three greatest problems

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.160
in physics were problems
that he never worked on

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.010
and really nobody else ever
worked on during his career.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:55.300
And those three problems were
time travel, teleportation,

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.720
and anti-gravity.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.739
And it's not that those weren't
problems for which a solution

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:01.780
would have had an impact.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.130
It would have actually
changed the world,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.080
and it would have given
you a reputation that

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:09.010
would have lasted centuries,
along with your Nobel Prize.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.677
But the problem is
nobody has a faintest

00:04:10.677 --> 00:04:12.093
idea how to attack
those problems,

00:04:12.093 --> 00:04:13.450
how to approach those problems.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.350
And therefore, it's a waste
of time working on them.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.190
So good problems have
the characteristic

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.000
that they are really
important in their impact,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.180
and you have some idea
how to tackle them.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.690
So if we bring this back
to the world of APIs,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.970
one very common way
to think about an API

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.280
and the purpose of
an API is that it's

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.750
a way that two
pieces of software

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.250
can communicate
across the network.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.710
I have a client and a server.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.260
And the client
needs to get at data

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.850
or to invoke actions
on the server.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.670
And we want the communication
to be fast, secure,

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.080
and easy to program.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.450
So many people come at APIs
with that as sort of the problem

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.026
definition.

00:04:57.026 --> 00:05:00.400
And that's actually not, at
least by Hamming's definition,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.260
an important problem
in our field.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.350
That doesn't mean that
it's not important.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.750
I've worked on many
projects where those were--

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.480
solving that problem
was important.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.560
But this is actually
a solved problem.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.120
There are several good
technologies for doing this.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.770
You just have to
pick one, if you

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:26.090
think that this is what
your primary problem is.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.060
Here are some different
goals that you might

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.360
have if you're defining an API.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.550
And these are important
problems in our field.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.550
And these are unsolved problems.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.430
So one important problem is that
software is just basically hard

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:41.200
to change.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.960
And every enterprise
that I've ever worked in

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.470
or every enterprise that I've
had contact with and worked

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.259
alongside with, which
is a much larger number,

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:52.550
has had the problem that they
are essentially prisoners

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.690
of their existing software.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.190
Unless you are in the first
few months of a startup,

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.510
you have a legacy of
existing software.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.160
And it's never what you want.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.010
It's never what you want
for your business needs.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.290
The business scenarios change.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:09.520
That business needs change.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.230
And it's never really what you
want from a technical point

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:12.100
of view either.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.720
Once you've done
something, you always

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:17.220
immediately learn how you
could have done it better.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.490
But you're stuck with it.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.280
And it's difficult to change
because it's complicated.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.350
And it's difficult to
change because in almost

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.270
all the systems that
we have, they're

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.770
permeated with assumptions.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.100
We have difficulty containing
the assumptions that we make,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.350
whether those are technical
assumptions about which

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.090
databases or which
technologies or which

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.360
implementation languages
we use or whether those

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.440
are business assumptions about
what actually the scenarios are

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.570
that the system has
to support, which

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:47.110
is also something that changes.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.790
Software is also very
hard to integrate.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:55.450
And integration is a huge
issue, especially in-- well,

00:06:55.450 --> 00:07:00.611
in all of the enterprises
that I have worked in.

00:07:00.611 --> 00:07:02.110
Almost all the
enterprises that I've

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:03.776
worked with have some
kind of initiative

00:07:03.776 --> 00:07:07.880
to get a 360-degree view of the
problem, which is basically,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.180
how do I integrate
all the information

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.710
that I have about a customer?

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:14.319
Or they're trying to integrate
the total experience.

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:16.360
They have many points of
contact with a customer,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:20.480
and they're trying to integrate
those things together.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.050
Many of you will have traveled
here to the conference.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.520
When you made your
booking, that's

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.700
a big integration app dealing
with hotel reservations,

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.660
your credit card, your flight
reservations, and things

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:32.540
like that.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.170
And when you go back and
do your expense report--

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.510
assuming that you have
a reasonably modern sort

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:40.192
of expense report tool, which
I'm sure most of you have--

00:07:40.192 --> 00:07:41.650
that's another big
integration app.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:45.730
It's integrating your
credit card bills.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.260
If it's a nice
modern application,

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.350
your flight receipt
and your hotel receipt

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.227
will already be known
in your expense report

00:07:52.227 --> 00:07:54.310
and associated with your
trip, that kind of thing.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.280
It's all a big kind
of integration app.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.090
So a huge amount of the
development that I see

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:04.280
is, in fact,
integration development.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:06.140
We build up these little
silos, and then we

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.720
realize that there's a
large amount of value

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.240
if we can integrate those silos.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.250
Software's hard to integrate
often because basic APIs are

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:17.300
not available.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.120
So it is difficult when
you can't get at the data

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.020
that you want to do.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.650
And also, because it's
an n-squared problem,

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.030
every system has
its own unique API.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.460
So if you're trying to
do an integration thing,

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.460
you get to learn the APIs
of all of the systems

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:37.280
that you're trying to integrate.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.330
I believe that
some styles of API

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:43.520
go beyond the first problem.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.350
They go beyond simply an
efficient, easy-to-program

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:52.160
communications between two
distributed components,

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.100
and they offer a
significant attack

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.301
on those two major problems.

00:08:56.301 --> 00:08:58.550
Of course, there are other
major problems in software.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.980
Like software quality
is a huge problem.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.510
But I don't have an
attack on that one,

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:04.800
so I left that off my list.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.740
But I think APIs
provide an attack

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.860
on those two major problems.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:14.180
There are two dominant
styles of API.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.630
One is the entity-oriented
style, sometimes called REST.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.360
I'll come back to
that word in a minute.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.060
And in that style,
the client code

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:28.100
manipulates the entities that
are exposed by the server,

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.410
and the behaviors
of the system are

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.240
hidden behind those entities.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.190
What I deal with is the
entities themselves.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:35.150
I can create entities.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.260
I can update entities.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:39.110
I can relate entities
to each other.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.030
What the system actually
does is a side effect

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:45.770
of those entity-based
sort of operations.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:49.970
An older model is the
procedure-oriented model,

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.560
the sort of RPC model.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.900
And that one is the
perfect inverse.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:10:00.110
In that one, what I interface
with is the procedures

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:01.640
that you provide.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.040
And the entities that those
procedures are talking about

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:05.990
are hidden behind
the procedures.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.490
So one puts the entities
in front of the behaviors.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.340
The other one puts the behaviors
in front of the entities.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:11.780
They're inverses of each other.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.534
Here's my kind of
stylized thing.

00:10:17.534 --> 00:10:19.700
And actually, you're probably
annoyed at this chart,

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:20.900
and I'm a little
annoyed at this chart.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.570
Because it's trying to make a
point which I actually believe

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.117
is true.

00:10:24.117 --> 00:10:25.700
And the point that
it's trying to make

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.870
is that in the
procedure-oriented style,

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.880
typically what
you end up with is

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.060
a very large number
of these procedures

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.890
that you have to master.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:40.370
So there's a lot of these
basic procedures or functions

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:45.230
that you call, that you
learn a long list of.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.690
The entity-oriented
style has more structure.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:50.630
And the problem with this
chart is that it's not really

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.800
balanced on each side.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.480
The procedures that
I'm showing actually

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.700
imply a number of
different entities.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:56.380
There's the dog.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.710
There is the owner of the dog.

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:02.240
There's the veterinary
surgeon that deals

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.530
with the health of the dog.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.350
So if this were
really a balanced one,

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.317
I would have multiple entities
on the left-hand side.

00:11:10.317 --> 00:11:11.900
But the point that
it's trying to make

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:13.520
is that there's
much more structure

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:14.550
on the left-hand side.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.360
The left-hand side is like an ER
Diagram, an Entity Relationship

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:18.290
Diagram.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.450
I understand what the
basic entities are.

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:22.710
I understand the
relationships between them.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.344
But the actual methods that
I call are very limited,

00:11:25.344 --> 00:11:26.510
and they're always the same.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.621
The procedure-oriented
style is very

00:11:31.621 --> 00:11:33.703
similar to learning the
libraries of a programming

00:11:33.703 --> 00:11:34.210
language.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:35.960
And I think this is
one of the reasons why

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.200
RPC has been perennially
popular with programmers,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.736
because it's very familiar.

00:11:40.736 --> 00:11:42.110
You learn a
programming language.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.930
You learn the libraries of
the programming language.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:48.980
And learning
distributed API that

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.320
is written in the
RPC style is just

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:52.820
like learning another library.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:55.190
It's not really very,
very different from that.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:57.860
But one of the characteristics
is all of those APIs

00:11:57.860 --> 00:11:59.780
are different.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.890
The entity-oriented style
is much more similar

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:06.200
to learning to use a database.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.616
So one of the characteristics
is if you use whatever

00:12:08.616 --> 00:12:09.740
your favorite database is--

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.750
MySQL, or Postgres, or
Oracle, or whatever it is--

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:18.020
a fundamental characteristic
is that there is only one API.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.930
It doesn't matter how
many databases you have.

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:23.540
The database management system
that hosts that database

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:27.170
defines a single API that's
used for all of them.

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.090
So you only ever learn one API.

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:31.460
Now, when you go from
database to database,

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.070
you have to learn the
schema of that database.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:38.330
You have to learn the tables
and what are the tables.

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:41.180
And then, you learn, what are
the columns in that table?

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.890
Or if you're using
MySQL database,

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.880
the terminology
changes a little bit,

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.990
but the concepts don't
change very radically.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:51.050
So the entity-oriented
approach has

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:53.360
this very interesting
characteristic

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.330
of solving the
n-squared problem.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:59.600
Instead of having an
individual API for each system,

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:04.464
I have one universal API that's
used with all of the systems.

00:13:04.464 --> 00:13:07.270
And I gave this talk--

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:10.234
I practiced this talk.

00:13:10.234 --> 00:13:12.150
It's not very common for
me to practice talks,

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:15.110
but Google wants us to do the
best that we can for you guys.

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:16.930
So I was encouraged
to practice this talk.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:18.030
And when I gave this
talk internally,

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.620
somebody said, are
you really telling

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.260
me that my API should
just show-- it should just

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.060
be a view onto my database?

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.194
And the answer is no,
that's not what I'm saying.

00:13:27.194 --> 00:13:28.860
What you actually
store in your database

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.400
is probably more complicated.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.756
You may have multiple
tables for each entity.

00:13:32.756 --> 00:13:34.630
You may not even have
tables for your entity.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.255
Your entities may be assembled
from information that you

00:13:37.255 --> 00:13:38.255
get from other services.

00:13:38.255 --> 00:13:41.250
So it's not about taking
your database schema

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.620
and just exposing
it as your API,

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.930
but it is about using the
same kind of concepts,

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:50.120
the same conceptual
model for an API

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.280
that you see used
for a database.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:57.950
So if we're going to attack
these important problems,

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:00.300
our problem was
it's hard to change,

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.030
and it's hard to integrate.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.980
Some of the
characteristics from APIs,

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.840
especially the
entity-oriented APIs that

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.180
will help us here is-- one
is having a uniform API

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.150
across all applications.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:13.777
That helps with our
n-squared problem.

00:14:13.777 --> 00:14:16.110
Another one is having a uniform
model for relationships.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.235
I'll talk a little bit more
about why relationships

00:14:18.235 --> 00:14:19.290
are so important in this.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.300
And then the third
one is staying free

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.250
of implementation assumptions.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:29.040
So how do we get that from this
kind of entity-oriented API?

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.220
I said I'd come back
to the REST term.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:34.850
REST API is actually a
little bit of an oxymoron,

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:41.640
because one of the primary rules
of REST is-- what was REST?

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:44.760
REST was one man's
attempt to write down

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:50.070
the architectural principles
that underpinned the HTTP web.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.530
So it's really a description
of the architectural style

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.810
or the architectural
principles of the HTTP web.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.080
And one of those principles
is uniform interface,

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:00.940
which means that it really
only is one interface.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:02.550
So when we talk
about our REST API,

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.370
there's only one important
REST API in the world,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.337
and that's HTTP.

00:15:07.337 --> 00:15:09.420
So when we talk about REST
APIs, what we're really

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:11.370
talking about is,
how do we just use

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:14.280
HTTP-- just use HTTP
exactly as it is

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.520
and apply it to
the domain of APIs?

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:19.350
Really what we're
saying is don't layer

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:20.820
concepts on top of HTTP.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:21.920
Just use it as is.

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:25.150
It is the universal API.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:28.350
Unfortunately, HTTP
has some gaps here.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:33.530
HTTP actually tells you how
to do crud, in a nice way.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:36.480
HTTP does not tell you how
to do query, for example.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:40.115
And query turns out to be very
important for almost all APIs.

00:15:40.115 --> 00:15:44.030
And neither does it tell
you how to do versioning.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:46.880
So there is some amount
of invention required

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:52.880
beyond the sort of universal API
that is provided to us by HTTP.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:56.180
How do you actually leverage
this uniform API, this HTTP

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:56.940
API?

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.032
Well, again, it's
a lot like what

00:15:59.032 --> 00:16:00.240
you would do with a database.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.656
The first thing you have to
do is you have to define a few

00:16:02.656 --> 00:16:03.620
well-known URLs--

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.230
for example, /customers,
/accounts, /orders,

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:09.050
which is very analogous to
defining what the tables are

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.050
if I'm defining a
database schema.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.120
And then, I have to define the
actual resource schema, which

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.010
is very analogous
to defining, what

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:20.720
are the columns for each
one of those entities?

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.660
And then the rest of the problem
is don't mess up the basics.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:27.100
So when we have to start adding
query and adding versioning

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:29.390
and things like
that, do it in a way

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:35.360
that's compatible with this
standard API that we want to--

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.430
that we want to show.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.450
So here's something
that's very typical.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:41.786
So here is a simplified version.

00:16:41.786 --> 00:16:45.784
And if you've worked at all
in sort of HTTP JSON APIs,

00:16:45.784 --> 00:16:47.450
I shouldn't think
there's very much here

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:50.420
that's not familiar to you.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:56.390
But the thing that I'm
highlighting is this id:12345.

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:58.370
And when you do
that, you've already

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:02.867
stepped outside the
uniform interface.

00:17:02.867 --> 00:17:04.700
And the reason is that
the uniform interface

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:07.420
that we're trying to use, the
HTTP interface, requires URLs.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:10.790
It doesn't require
simple numeric IDs.

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:16.130
So if I'm going to write
my identifiers this way,

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:21.756
I'm going to require extra
information about my API.

00:17:21.756 --> 00:17:23.839
I'm going to have to
describe in the documentation

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:27.500
how you take one of those
IDs and how you transform it

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:31.460
into a URL that can
then be used in the API.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:34.820
And this was unfortunate,
because it would have been just

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:37.290
as easy to do that.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:40.520
And if I'd done that
instead, then now,

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:42.230
I stay within the uniform API.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:46.400
I don't require you to have
extra out-of-band information

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:53.600
about my API in order to use the
universal API, which is HTTP.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:57.770
So very small change,
but these things

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:01.610
sort of make a difference
when they're accumulated.

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:03.380
Relationships are
extremely important.

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:04.730
Relationships are
extremely important when

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.604
you're designing a
database, and they're also

00:18:06.604 --> 00:18:10.910
extremely important when
you're designing an API.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:15.980
One of the reasons
is that APIs--

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:20.030
the fundamental problem in
most integration projects

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.040
is creating relationships
between entities

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:25.460
that are stored in
different applications.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:28.070
Sometimes, I'm trying
to get app function

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.740
through a particular API.

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:32.880
The integration examples
that I gave earlier--

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:36.590
for example, your expense
reports or your travel booking,

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:38.780
are all about relating
entities to each other.

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:45.590
I'm taking this flight along
with this hotel reservation.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.830
And I'm linking them all
together with this trip ID.

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:54.050
So I'm relating entities
in different systems.

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:56.300
Relationships are a
very fundamental way

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:57.259
about the way we think.

00:18:57.259 --> 00:18:58.633
Thinking about
the world in terms

00:18:58.633 --> 00:19:00.320
of entities and
relationships is an idea

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.025
that goes at least back
to Plato and Aristotle.

00:19:03.025 --> 00:19:04.760
I put their pictures
here just in case

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.720
you come across them
at the conference

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.180
and want to talk to them.

00:19:13.850 --> 00:19:15.590
Relationships
depend on identity.

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:17.780
I cannot reason about a
relationship unless I can

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:19.280
reason about identity.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:22.400
I can't say John loves
Mary unless I can reliably

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:26.645
identify John and
reliably identify Mary.

00:19:26.645 --> 00:19:29.120
And so one of the
primary obstacles

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:32.510
to these kind of
relationship-based integrations

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:36.260
through APIs is the struggle
that we have with identity.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.800
And here, the universal
API, the HTTP API,

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:44.750
makes a huge contribution by
defining the concept of a URI--

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:48.350
or a URL, which gives
us the ability to,

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:51.980
across all of our
systems, write identifiers

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:56.630
in a way that's standard and
repeatable and storable and all

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:59.340
of those kind of things.

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:03.050
But again, we tend to
make mistakes here.

00:20:03.050 --> 00:20:07.537
So here's a particularly--

00:20:07.537 --> 00:20:08.870
I've got the wrong pointer here.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:12.620
[INAUDIBLE] Didn't
mean to do that.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.920
So here, focus on
this last line.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:20.350
So this is a very typical
way that you'll see people

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:24.520
writing or attempting
to write relationships

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:27.550
in a typical sort of JSON API.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:28.720
They write ownerID.

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:33.610
OwnerID is the field that I've
used to capture the owner.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:35.850
And I give its
numeric identifier.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:38.616
Now, the problem with this
is that this identifier,

00:20:38.616 --> 00:20:40.240
I need a lot of
contextual information.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:43.540
I cannot just use the uniform
API to follow this link,

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:46.180
because I need
contextual information.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.840
I need to know
which other systems

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:51.490
can I put this value into?

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:53.860
And how do I plug that
in a way that creates

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:57.400
a URL that I can then use?

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.805
And in fact, this breaks
down in different ways.

00:20:59.805 --> 00:21:01.930
I mean, supposing I had
different kinds of owners--

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:05.810
you know, some owners
are police dogs, right?

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:08.860
Or rather, police dogs have
a different kind of owner.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:11.594
In that case, the owner of
the dog might not be a person.

00:21:11.594 --> 00:21:13.510
It might be the police
department or something

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:14.009
like that.

00:21:14.009 --> 00:21:18.550
So how do I know to
resolve this number

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:23.830
by looking in the system of
institutional owners as opposed

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:26.270
to personal owners,
those kind of things?

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:28.930
And we could have avoided
all of those problems

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:30.980
if we'd simply
written it this way.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:34.300
In other words, owner's the
relationship, and give a URL.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.330
If the URL's in the same system,
you can write it relatively.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:40.300
I can resolve a
relative URL knowing

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:43.990
only the standard specifications
published by IETF.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:47.710
I don't have to know anything
specific about your URLs

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:49.660
or your systems to do that.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:54.190
Or if this references
something in another system,

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:57.620
then I write the URL
in its full form.

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:00.190
And you might think about how
do you do this kind of example

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:00.920
with RPC?

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.950
With RPC, it's probably
even more complicated.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:04.908
With RPC, you have to
take something like this.

00:22:04.908 --> 00:22:07.930
And then, you have
to know, amongst all

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:09.880
of the remote procedures
that I could call,

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.860
which are the ones that
will take something

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:13.750
like this as an argument?

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:19.670
So having a uniform
model for identities

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.830
is extremely important.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.090
And the simplest way
to do that, to leverage

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:28.970
the universal interface, HTTP,
is make every idea a URL.

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:30.480
Another thing you
should do is not

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.780
to confuse identity with name.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:35.210
So thinking about identity
is very important.

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:38.190
It's very important if you're
going to make this thing work.

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:39.750
So think carefully
about identity,

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:43.070
and don't confuse
identities with names.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:44.990
So what is a name?

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.150
Oh, here's a nice description
of what names mean

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:51.220
in English going back to 1597.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.571
This is "Romeo and Juliet."

00:22:53.571 --> 00:22:54.820
You all know the story, right?

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.820
In fact, this is one of
the most famous passages

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:59.420
in all of the English language.

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:03.710
And so Juliet would have
made a terrific API designer.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:09.090
She thinks very carefully and
very clearly about concepts.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:10.610
Now, what she's
saying to Romeo here

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:12.320
is that your name
doesn't really matter.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:15.257
Your name does not
say who you are.

00:23:15.257 --> 00:23:17.840
And you can cast off your name
if your name's not convenient--

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:22.100
you know, "doff
thy name," Romeo--

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:24.860
which is very different
from your identity.

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:27.380
And so names are terrifically
important things.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.130
They do have a place in APIs.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:31.640
Many well-designed
APIs support names.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:34.730
But they do not confuse
names with identities.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:38.630
So here's an example.

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:40.120
Here's a good identity.

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:41.720
This is a pretty good identity.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.430
This is a very simple--

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.920
doesn't matter what kind of
what kind of resource it is.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:49.640
No amount of change--

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:55.090
this pet-- well, maybe there is
a problem with this, actually.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.430
So for example, if
I have a goat that's

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:02.944
part of a herd that's really
just one of my possessions,

00:24:02.944 --> 00:24:05.110
and then I take pity on one
of these poor sick goats

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:07.090
and take it into my
house, it became a pet.

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:08.291
So something changed here.

00:24:08.291 --> 00:24:09.790
So this actually
might not be a pet,

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:12.160
might not be a
very good identity,

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:14.230
because it refers to
a characteristic that

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:15.340
might actually change.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:18.220
Something that was
a pet might not

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.850
be a pet if it's just put out in
a field with the other animals.

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:23.530
The important things
about identities

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:25.170
is that they are immutable.

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:26.860
They're not based on
some characteristic

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.600
that might change.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.450
I see lots of people
trying to make

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:33.275
identities that look like this.

00:24:33.275 --> 00:24:35.650
And it's very attractive,
because this kind of identity--

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:36.858
one, two, three, four, five--

00:24:36.858 --> 00:24:38.580
nobody can remember that.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.870
But you can remember
that Lassie was

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:44.280
a dog, if you remember the
Lassie story from the movie.

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:47.440
Lassie, though, was a dog that
belonged to Joe Carraclough.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:51.710
Unfortunately, Lassie was
taken away by the Duke of--

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:53.020
I forget, whatever.

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:55.360
So not a very good
identity, because Lassie

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:58.420
changed ownership in the movie.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:00.550
But it is useful to
have URLs like this,

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:03.550
because it's very useful to be
able to look things up by name.

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:07.780
But you have to be aware
that names can change.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.400
And in fact, that
brings us to query.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:11.233
So one of the things
that I warned you about

00:25:11.233 --> 00:25:16.210
is basing APIs on HTTP is
a very powerful technique,

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:21.250
but there are gaps in
HTTP, particularly around

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.440
how you deal with query.

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:28.660
And in fact, this URL, which is
basically saying, find the dog,

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:33.157
or the pet, called Lassie that
belongs to Joe Carraclough,

00:25:33.157 --> 00:25:34.240
can be rewritten this way.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:35.980
It's really just a
stylistic difference.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:37.850
But both of these are queries.

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:39.850
This is not a URI in the sense.

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:43.920
Well, it's a URI of a
search, not the URI of a dog.

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:47.519
And if you write
that URL this way,

00:25:47.519 --> 00:25:49.060
it doesn't really
change the meaning.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:51.070
But it does make it a
little bit more obvious

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:55.810
that, in fact, this is
just the URL of a query.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:57.730
It's not that URL
of a particular dog.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:25:59.590
And because it's
a query, it's not

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:01.630
guaranteed to get the
same result twice.

00:26:05.267 --> 00:26:06.600
Let's talk about representation.

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:09.600
So representation
in HTTP is the word

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:12.735
that is used to mean the
actual format of the resources.

00:26:12.735 --> 00:26:16.380
So if I do get on a particular
resource, what I get back

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.070
is the representation.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:22.890
In the HTTP world, JSON is the
most common sort of language

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:27.270
that we use for writing
down those representations.

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.970
But one of our design
problems as an API designer

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:31.990
is, what should
the JSON look like?

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:34.350
And there are lots of options.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:37.030
If your focus is the first
focus that I talked about,

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:39.760
that your primary reason
for building an API

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:41.850
is for efficient
communication between two

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.615
components on the
network, then the kinds

00:26:43.615 --> 00:26:45.240
of things that you're
going to focus on

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.190
is you want things
that are efficient.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:48.827
You want things that
are easy to parse.

00:26:48.827 --> 00:26:50.910
You want a good match for
the programming language

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:52.020
on each end.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:58.080
Those are going to be your
sort of your primary values.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:03.390
If your focus is, how do I
make things easy to integrate,

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:07.350
and how do I make things
easy to change in the future,

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:08.910
your focus is going
to be different.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.620
Your focus is actually
going to be independence

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.210
from technology,
because you may want

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:14.880
to anticipate different
clients of the same API that

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.910
will show up at different
points in history.

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:22.110
And resilience to change is
much more important to you

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:23.850
than things like
ease of parsing.

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:26.880
And sometimes, these
are in conflict.

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:30.746
So you may have to make up your
mind what's important to you.

00:27:30.746 --> 00:27:33.120
If you're working directly
with the implementation teams,

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:34.170
they have a lot of biases.

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:35.753
And they will push
for certain things.

00:27:35.753 --> 00:27:38.520
They won't like homogeneous
collections, for example.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.350
They want things that
are easy to parse.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.330
And especially if you're working
in a typed language, like Java

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.172
or Go or something like that,
it's easier to parse things.

00:27:46.172 --> 00:27:48.630
It's not impossible to parse
things if you don't have this,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.671
but it's easier to parse
things if you know ahead

00:27:50.671 --> 00:27:52.410
of time what the types will be.

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:53.840
So don't give me
a collection that

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:56.580
has a lot of different
types of things in it.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:00.750
I don't want the famous
"Sound of Music"--

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:03.150
you know, "My Favorite
Things," "Raindrops on roses

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:04.660
and whiskers on kittens."

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:06.660
So those are all
different kinds of things.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:08.410
Don't put them all in
the same collection,

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:11.410
because I find that
harder to parse.

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:14.820
Closed content-- don't tell
me that you don't know ahead

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:17.280
of time what all the fields are.

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:19.486
Because in my parsing
routines, I really

00:28:19.486 --> 00:28:22.110
like to know what all the fields
are going to be ahead of time.

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.140
So you'll get these
kind of things.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:26.640
I'll show you what I mean
by indexed collections.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:29.220
Union types are unpopular.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.320
Don't give me a field
that's sometimes

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.069
a string and
sometimes an object.

00:28:33.069 --> 00:28:34.110
I don't really like that.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:35.640
Makes it harder for me to parse.

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:38.370
So you get lots of these
kind of constraints

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:43.410
coming from developers of things
that they don't really like.

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:45.840
As an API designer,
if your goals

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:48.101
are the big problems
that I talked about,

00:28:48.101 --> 00:28:50.350
I think you have to push
back on some of these things.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:52.240
Some of these things are fine.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:53.724
It's not always a
good idea to have

00:28:53.724 --> 00:28:55.140
a single variable
that's sometimes

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:58.060
a string and sometimes a number.

00:28:58.060 --> 00:29:01.110
But if you think it makes
conceptual sense, then

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:05.010
maybe you should push back on
the implementation teams who've

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:06.570
got specific constraints.

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:09.974
Here's an example of what I
mean by indexed collection bias.

00:29:09.974 --> 00:29:10.890
And this is actually--

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.110
I copied this out of
an old Facebook API.

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:14.220
This may not be current.

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:18.690
But this is a Facebook API
for listing photographs.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.610
And you can see the way
the data is organized

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.611
is actually reasonably
complicated when

00:29:22.611 --> 00:29:23.610
I do it with my pointer.

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:33.220
What happens is that the
outer name in the JSON thing

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.080
is actually the user ID.

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:37.570
And then, the inner
stuff is a set

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:41.090
of photographs for that user.

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:43.690
And then, we start with
a second user here.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:46.600
And then we have a block
that's the list of photographs

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:48.190
for that user.

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:53.800
And when you design JSON this
way, what you're really saying

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:56.530
is that you're favoring
one particular access

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:58.330
pattern for the data.

00:29:58.330 --> 00:30:03.250
You're assuming that the
primary usage by the client

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:07.060
will be that they'll want to
access the photographs based

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:08.390
on the user ID.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:11.460
If they wanted to access
the photograph based

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:13.650
on creation time or
something like that,

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:17.160
this is actually not a very
friendly data structure.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.510
So within this data
structure, you've

00:30:20.510 --> 00:30:25.160
sort of advantaged one
set of access patterns

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:30.410
and disadvantaged another
set, which might be OK,

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:33.160
but it might have been better to
do something simpler like this.

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:34.327
Flatten the whole thing out.

00:30:34.327 --> 00:30:36.910
So if I had flattened the whole
thing out-- well first of all,

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:39.170
it's simpler, because
it's much flatter.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:42.260
And what I really did was
I took these user IDs,

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:45.170
and I moved them into the block.

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:48.110
This is going to be
less convenient to use

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:53.810
if my primary access method is
accessing the data by user ID.

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:56.030
But if I'm accessing it
by some other criteria,

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:58.140
it's actually better.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:30:59.880
I actually prefer this.

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:01.520
So I prefer the second form.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.460
Because I'm more open to change.

00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:07.280
If I have different users that
have different access patterns,

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:09.770
I haven't penalized
the second set of users

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:12.050
because I optimized
my data representation

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:14.510
for the first set of users,
even though the first set

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:16.220
of programmers were
probably pushing

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.524
me to do something like this.

00:31:18.524 --> 00:31:19.940
Because they wanted
something that

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:21.990
was convenient to
their access pattern.

00:31:26.436 --> 00:31:28.590
I'm very public-spirited.

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:32.790
And I have taken the
trouble to write down

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:33.919
a set of possible rules.

00:31:33.919 --> 00:31:36.210
I'm not claiming that these
are the only rules that you

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:41.410
can-- that need to follow if
you're going to use HTTP well.

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:43.170
But I tried to write
down a set of rules

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:46.200
that explain how you can
use JSON in, perhaps,

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:52.440
the most simple form possible
that might be helpful.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:56.350
And the reason I like it
is it's more abstract.

00:31:56.350 --> 00:32:00.250
It's less geared towards one
particular access pattern.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:06.420
So query's not
dealt with by HTTP.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:07.740
Neither is versioning.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:11.100
And versioning is a
very confusing topic.

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:14.400
One reason is there's at least
three meanings of versioning.

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.590
And it's very
common when you hear

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:19.440
people talking about
versioning for people

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:21.622
to talk past each
other, because they're

00:32:21.622 --> 00:32:23.580
each thinking about
versioning a different way.

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:26.310
They're each thinking about a
different aspect of versioning.

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:28.920
And you sometimes find people
even getting themselves

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:30.540
confused by using
the word versioning

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:32.290
in two different ways
in the same sentence

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:34.020
and getting muddled.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:37.350
So one kind of versioning,
I have dubbed here

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:40.945
entity versioning, which says,
there are multiple versions.

00:32:40.945 --> 00:32:42.870
So I have a particular
kind of an entity.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.910
I have a travel
trip or something,

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.030
to stick with our
previous example.

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:51.300
And I introduce version
one of travel trip.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.500
And then, that was successful
for a couple of years.

00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:56.100
And then, I had a major upgrade,
and we completely reworked

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:57.240
our data model.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:59.070
Because we reworked
our data model

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:02.220
to accommodate some
more advanced scenarios,

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:10.810
the old trips and the
new trips are different.

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:13.410
So I introduced a
version two of my system,

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:15.540
but when I introduced
version two,

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:17.250
I started creating
version two trips.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:19.840
So every trip is either a
version one trip or a version

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:21.840
two trip, but they're not
both at the same time.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.440
So they're a different
set of entities.

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:24.856
So that's a very
common thing that

00:33:24.856 --> 00:33:29.620
happens when you make a major
change to your data model.

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:32.334
Another kind of
versioning is what

00:33:32.334 --> 00:33:34.500
I have dubbed-- this is
just a personal vocabulary--

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.870
but I have dubbed
format versioning.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:39.480
And what happened there
was I introduced trips,

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:42.240
and I gave them one
JSON representation.

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:45.330
And then I realized from
feedback from my customers,

00:33:45.330 --> 00:33:48.060
they didn't like the names that
I chose for the properties,

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:51.510
or they didn't like the way
that I organized the JSON.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:54.177
Maybe from the previous example,
I'd organized it the first way,

00:33:54.177 --> 00:33:56.510
and they really preferred I
organized it the second way,

00:33:56.510 --> 00:33:57.610
or the other way around.

00:33:57.610 --> 00:33:59.910
So I decided to
introduce a version two,

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:02.160
but the important thing
is that each trip can

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:05.340
be viewed either as through
the version one format

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:06.904
or the version two format.

00:34:06.904 --> 00:34:08.570
They're not two
different sets of trips.

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:09.986
They're the same
trips that can be

00:34:09.986 --> 00:34:12.040
viewed in two different ways.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.460
The third kind of versioning
that you hear about

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.230
is where you keep prior
revisions of the same thing.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:19.440
So if I have a document,
a Google Doc, for example,

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:21.449
and I keep changing
my Google Doc,

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:27.710
I may have a long revision
history for that thing.

00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:30.239
And you use these
in different places.

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:31.920
You use entity
versioning if you made

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:34.050
a significant structural
change to your data model.

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.750
You use format
versioning if you're

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:38.730
making small changes
to the representation

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:39.929
of individual entities.

00:34:39.929 --> 00:34:41.880
And you use
historical versioning

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:44.770
if you're doing things like
creating an audit trail,

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:45.810
undoing undo/redo.

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:48.250
And they're all different.

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:50.730
So having got clarity
on that, let's look

00:34:50.730 --> 00:34:52.320
at format versioning,
because that's

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:56.550
the one that I think people
do most commonly with APIs.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:59.920
And let's look at some of
the mistakes that they make.

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:01.590
So I'm doing format versioning.

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:05.640
So one thing that you'll see
people commonly doing is this.

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:09.060
They put a version
number in a URL

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:13.200
that they then use to
try to make a link.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:15.314
And that doesn't
really work well.

00:35:15.314 --> 00:35:16.980
And the reason that
it doesn't work well

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:21.270
is that just because I
wanted version one of Lassie

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:26.360
doesn't not mean that I want
version one of Lassie's owner.

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:28.020
And in fact, there
may not even be

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.790
a version-- let's say I asked
for version two of Lassie.

00:35:30.790 --> 00:35:33.400
There may not even be a
version two of Lassie's owner.

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:35.640
Owners may only
have a version one.

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:39.630
So it's not possible
for the server

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:44.950
to select the version
to use in a link.

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:47.570
So what that means is you've
got one or two choices.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:50.500
Either you don't put version
numbers in links at all.

00:35:50.500 --> 00:35:51.830
That's one possibility.

00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:53.620
So that will solve the problem.

00:35:53.620 --> 00:35:57.947
Or for each entity, you
need to provide a URL,

00:35:57.947 --> 00:36:00.280
one that does have the version
number in it and one that

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:01.130
doesn't.

00:36:01.130 --> 00:36:03.850
And the one that does not
have the version number

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:05.950
is the one that you
will use in the links.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:09.419
And then, customers or
clients can transform that

00:36:09.419 --> 00:36:11.710
into a URL that does have a
version number if they want

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:13.540
to select a particular version.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.220
So either of the
strategies will work.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:20.650
But what will not
work is trying to use

00:36:20.650 --> 00:36:24.740
URLs that have version
IDs in them in links.

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:31.000
So you might protest
that if I do this--

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:32.840
OK, let's say I take
all of this stuff out.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:35.110
So I've taken all the
version numbers, or version

00:36:35.110 --> 00:36:37.199
identifiers, out of the URLs.

00:36:37.199 --> 00:36:38.740
But then, I've lost
some information.

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:41.680
Well, you can put
it back if you like.

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:44.360
Invent a property instead for--

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:46.458
my pointer just stopped working.

00:36:46.458 --> 00:36:48.234
Has it?

00:36:48.234 --> 00:36:49.130
Yes.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:52.300
Invent a property that tells
you which version number--

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.285
or which version
format you're looking

00:36:54.285 --> 00:36:56.100
at when you're looking at this.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:37:00.210
And if you want
to give out an ID

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:03.830
and also tell me exactly
which version I'm

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:06.330
looking at of the current one
or URL of the current version,

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:07.663
you can use something like this.

00:37:07.663 --> 00:37:11.040
And these values
actually probably

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:12.670
fit better with
the style of HTTP

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:14.250
if we put these in headers,
but there's certainly

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:15.999
no harm in having them
in properties also.

00:37:17.949 --> 00:37:20.490
A lot of what we're doing here
is just not messing things up,

00:37:20.490 --> 00:37:21.210
right?

00:37:21.210 --> 00:37:24.292
We've got this universal
API called HTTP.

00:37:24.292 --> 00:37:25.250
We know how to work it.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:27.300
It works based on URLs.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:28.920
Just follow the pattern.

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:31.530
So as you are designing
things and you're

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:34.500
having to add things that
are missing out of HTTP,

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:37.890
do it in a way that doesn't
mess up the basics of how

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:40.830
HTTP is actually used.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.330
So the right way
of doing versioning

00:37:42.330 --> 00:37:46.050
is every entity has a
version-free identity URI.

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:48.690
You can allow clients to use
the version-free identity

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:52.410
URI directly by having
an accept version header.

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:55.680
Or you can allow them
to transform the URL

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:59.802
into a different
URL that does have

00:37:59.802 --> 00:38:01.010
the version identifier in it.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.426
If you do that,
then you're having

00:38:02.426 --> 00:38:06.140
to provide basically a
transformation algorithm that's

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:07.610
specific to your API.

00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:11.600
It's not a generic part
of the protocol anymore.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:16.650
So I'm going to wrap up here
and invite you to ask questions.

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:18.830
But my conclusion
slide is decide

00:38:18.830 --> 00:38:20.280
what you really care about.

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:24.500
If you care primarily-- in
this particular application

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:26.167
that you're doing,
if you care primarily

00:38:26.167 --> 00:38:28.375
about efficient communication
between tightly-coupled

00:38:28.375 --> 00:38:30.680
components, there are lots
of great choices out there.

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:33.470
GRPC is the one that's
favored by Google,

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:35.060
and it's a great choice.

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:39.740
If what you're focused on
is latency and throughput

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:43.280
and efficiency and
ease of programming,

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.650
RPC does all of those
things very well.

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:48.830
If you're aiming for something
more abstract that you're

00:38:48.830 --> 00:38:51.830
creating your APIs
because you're primarily

00:38:51.830 --> 00:38:54.320
interested in decoupling
systems so that they're

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:56.480
more adaptable to
change in the future,

00:38:56.480 --> 00:39:00.830
or you're primarily interested
in creating APIs that will

00:39:00.830 --> 00:39:03.429
enable integration scenarios--
and actually one of the most

00:39:03.429 --> 00:39:04.970
important integration
scenarios which

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:08.090
I failed to mention
earlier is the one

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:12.590
when you're publishing an API
to your partners or customers.

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:17.210
So if you're creating APIs
that will be consumed only

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:19.580
internally within
your own-- or you

00:39:19.580 --> 00:39:21.530
think will only be
consumed internally

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:24.260
within your own
organization, it may

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:28.310
be that the top set of
concerns might be the ones that

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:31.340
dominate your thinking.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:35.950
Although, actually, I would
challenge that, but anyway.

00:39:35.950 --> 00:39:38.810
But if you're creating APIs that
will be used by other people,

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:41.240
that will be consumed by
development organizations

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:44.060
over which you have no control--

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:48.170
you can't control the rhythm
at which they change things.

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:51.710
You can't control the way in
which they version things.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:54.680
And you cannot anticipate all
the different ways in which

00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:56.630
they will access your data.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:59.090
If that is what
you're designing for,

00:39:59.090 --> 00:40:04.430
my recommendation is
don't use an RPC model.

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:05.960
Use an entity-oriented model.

00:40:05.960 --> 00:40:10.760
And in particular, use HTTP
JSON as directly and as simply

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:15.410
as possible, requiring as little
as possible extra information

00:40:15.410 --> 00:40:19.550
about your specific API that
they cannot glean directly from

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:22.016
the internet standards.

00:40:22.016 --> 00:40:25.520
So that's my talk.

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:27.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:31.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

